  "description": "issue #682 (slice I3): a handler block is a normal OpenLogo block (spec/interaction-events.md, \"Time, ticks, and handlers\"), so it runs in its REGISTRATION-TIME lexical scope. A `when \"start\"` handler registered inside `define setup :x` sees the parameter `:x` when it runs: `setup 7` registers the handler while `:x` is bound to `7`, and because `\"start\"` is already being delivered the handler fires immediately inside that procedure frame, printing `7` (seq 7). The `WhenHandler` captures the environment at registration for exactly this reason; without it the body would resolve `:x` against the wrong frame. Note the handler-block-start `instruction` (seq 5) carries the `when` keyword's span, distinct from the registration statement's full span (seq 3-4).",
